  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Ensuring the Delivery of Critical Humanitarian Assistance to Vulnerable Migrants in the Dominican Republic and The Bahamas</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Plunged into a long-lasting political, economic and social crisis, Haiti has become a major source country of emigrants, which over the years has resulted in a significant diaspora and a migrant population living in neighbouring host countries such as The Bahamas and the Dominican Republic. As a direct consequence of the lack of regular pathways for migration and limited socioeconomic opportunities in their country of origin, Haitian migrants regularly face a wide range of protection issues, including human trafficking, smuggling, communicable diseases as well as a lack of reintegration assistance for returnees and deportees. In close coordination with government authorities, this project aims to strengthen access to critical humanitarian assistance for vulnerable migrant communities, including Haitians, in the Dominican Republic and The Bahamas. To achieve this objective, the International Organization for Migration is facilitating access to critical humanitarian assistance for Haitian migrants in vulnerable situations in the Dominican Republic as well as migrants and communities at risk of displacement in The Bahamas.</narrative>
  </description>
